+
+### 0.4 A deterministic pre-existing failure, in this arc's own subject matter.
+
+`cargo test --test it` is NOT green on v11. The arc's stated baseline (962
+passed, 0 failed) does not reproduce on a clean tree.
+
+`flow_xlang::spec_op_resolves_across_languages` fails deterministically:
+
+```
+git cat-file failed for 24782c3d…:rust/src/handler.rs (… missing)
+```
+
+RCA. `read_content` (`src/engine/mod.rs:1131-1137`) picks the read path from the
+rev TEXT alone, and its doc comment (mod.rs:1118-1122) claims this is sound
+because "a rev WITHOUT the `+` marker is byte-identical on disk and in the git
+object". The claim holds only when `root` IS the repo root. The test builds
+`Engine::new(conn, <repo>/bench/flow)`, so:
+
+- `resolve_rev` shells `git -C bench/flow rev-parse HEAD`, getting the *sprefa*
+  repo's HEAD. On a clean tree there is no `+`.
+- `read_content` therefore takes the git-object branch, and calls
+  `git -C bench/flow cat-file <oid>:rust/src/handler.rs`.
+- git resolves `<oid>:<path>` relative to the REPO ROOT, not to `-C`. The correct
+  path is `bench/flow/rust/src/handler.rs`.
+
+Confirmed directly:
+
+```
+$ git -C bench/flow cat-file -e <oid>:rust/src/handler.rs
+fatal: path 'rust/src/handler.rs' does not exist in '<oid>'
+$ git -C bench/flow cat-file -e <oid>:bench/flow/rust/src/handler.rs
+(exists)
+```
+
+The defect is masked on the main working tree because that tree is dirty, so the
+rev carries `+` and the filesystem branch is taken. It surfaces on any clean
+checkout. It is exactly a `(repo, rev)` identity bug: the scan root is conflated
+with the repo root, and the resolved rev is not qualified by the repo it came
+from. Snapshot identity is the structural fix (section 3.4).
+
+Two further failures in the same full-suite run,
+`daemon::source_burst_coalesces_into_one_tick` and
+`db_ratio::daemon_boot_emits_db_ratio_for_the_served_root`, both PASS when run
+in isolation and did not recur on a second full run (6 failed, then 3 failed).
+They are load-sensitive flakes, not regressions.
+
+The greenness of that test is a function of working-tree cleanliness, not of
+correctness. One environment variable flips it, on the same commit:
+
+```
+$ DL_REV_OVERRIDE=24782c3d…                 # clean, no marker
+test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 2 failed
+$ DL_REV_OVERRIDE=24782c3d…+                # dirty marker
+test result: ok. 2 passed; 0 failed
+```
+
+This also explains the arc's quoted baseline of "962 passed, 0 failed": that
+number was measured on a dirty working tree, where every scan takes the
+filesystem branch and the git-object path is never exercised.
+
+Measured on this worktree:
+
+| suite | baseline (clean tree, no edits) | after stage 1 |
+|---|---|---|
+| `cargo test --lib` | 628 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored | **645 passed, 0 failed**, 1 ignored (17 new) |
+| `cargo test --test it` | 959 passed, **3 failed**, 36 ignored (run 1: 956/6) | **962 passed, 0 failed**, 36 ignored |
+| `dl .dl/ --parse-only` | exit 0 | exit 0 |
