+## 2. Instance lifetimes
+
+| type | holds | lifetime | cleared when |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `SnapshotId` | nothing; a `u64` value | VALUE type, copied freely, no state | n/a |
+| `SnapshotSink` | `Vec<(id, repo, rev)>` queued this pass | ONE per refresh pass, stack-local, mirrors `SymSink` exactly | drained by `Db::flush_snapshots`; debug-panics if dropped with pending entries unflushed (the `SymSink` silent-loss guard, restated) |
+| `_snapshot` table | every (repo, rev) ever stamped in this database | as long as the database file | never on a normal tick. Swept by the same reachability pass that sweeps `_strings` (`daemon/gc.rs:252`), not by any rel rebuild |
+| `WorktreeRev` (existing, `revid.rs`) | resolved worktree rev per repo root | ONE per `Engine`; cleared at each tick top and on a git event | unchanged by this arc |
+
+The critical lifetime statement: `_snapshot` rows OUTLIVE the rel rows that point
+at them, deliberately. A repo-scoped delete removes rel rows and leaves the
+`_snapshot` row standing, so a re-scan of the same (repo, rev) re-mints the same
+id — the id is a content hash, so this is automatic, not a lookup.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Storage layout, then reads and writes, then uniqueness
+
+### 3.1 Layout
+
+```sql
+CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS _snapshot (
+    id   INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,   -- SnapshotId::sqlite(), a rowid alias
+    repo INTEGER NOT NULL,      -- StringId::of(repo_slug).sqlite()
+    rev  INTEGER NOT NULL       -- StringId::of(rev_text).sqlite()
+);
+CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx__snapshot_repo ON _snapshot(repo);
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx__snapshot_pair ON _snapshot(repo, rev);
+```
+
+Three integer columns, no text. `repo`/`rev` carry `StringId::of` hashes with no
+`REFERENCES _strings`, for the reason `_extract_digest` already documents at
+`meta.rs:222-227`: the hash is computed without interning, and `PRAGMA
+foreign_keys` is ON for some connections.
+
+`id` is an `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` (rowid alias) rather than the `WITHOUT ROWID`
+composite that `_extract_digest` uses, and this is a deliberate divergence from
+the model the arc named. `_extract_digest` is keyed BY its composite and looked
+up by it. `_snapshot` is looked up by `id` on every join (the hot direction,
+which wants the rowid alias) and by `repo` only on the delete path (which the
+secondary index serves). A `WITHOUT ROWID` table keyed `(repo, rev)` would make
+the hot direction a secondary-index hop. The uniqueness the composite PK would
+have given is preserved by `idx__snapshot_pair`.
+
+The rel-side column, for each rel that carries a coordinate:
+
+```
+snap INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0     -- Type::Snap, SnapshotId::sqlite()
+```
+
+Storage-shape-identical to today's `Type::Rev` column, which is already an
+interned `StringId` stored as INTEGER (`Col::interned` at `ast.rs:64`,
+`Col::sql` at `ast.rs:65-67`). This is why the migration is mechanical: the
+column's SQLite type does not change, only what the integer means.
+
+### 3.2 Sequence of reads and writes, one tick
+
+```
+resolve_scan_bindings            repo.rs:297
+  -> resolve_repo / resolve_scan_repos     yields (slug, root)
+  -> resolve_rev                           yields RevId
+  -> snap = sink.intern(&slug, &rev)       MINT HERE, repo.rs:334 and repo.rs:407
+  -> ScanBinding { slug, root, rev, snap, glob, head_binds }
+
+reconcile.rs:44  iterates bindings
+  -> enumerate_with_hash(slug, root, rev, ...)     scan.rs:128, unchanged
+  -> per file: read_content(root, rev, path)       see 3.4
+  -> eval.rs:400-420 seeds the Bind with path/rev/head_binds
+       ADD: seed `snap` alongside, so a rule head can reference it
+
+flush
+  -> db.flush_syms(&mut syms)          existing, one statement
+  -> db.flush_snapshots(&mut sink)     NEW, two statements
+  -> db.insert_rows(rel, cols, rows)   existing, one statement per rel
+
+rebuild
+  -> reload_rel_for_snapshot(...)      replaces reload_rel's unscoped wipe
+```
+
+The mint point matters and is the whole design: at `repo.rs:334` and
+`repo.rs:407` the pair `(slug, rev)` is already sitting in one struct
+literal. The two halves are together in memory throughout extraction — `eval.rs`
+takes `repo` and `path` as ambient parameters right through the `Match`/`Ast`/
+`Sg` ops — and only split at the storage boundary. Interning at the binding is
+therefore not a new coupling; it is refusing to discard one that already exists.
+
+### 3.3 Uniqueness conditions
+
+1. `_snapshot.id` is unique by PK. Enforced.
+2. `(repo, rev)` is unique by `idx__snapshot_pair`. Enforced.
+3. `id` is a FUNCTION of `(repo, rev)`, by content hash. NOT enforced by the
+   schema; guaranteed by `SnapshotId::of` being the only constructor and by the
+   `INSERT OR IGNORE` never rewriting an existing row. A test pins it: minting
+   the same pair twice in two processes yields the same integer.
+4. `SnapshotId::NONE` (0) is never inserted into `_snapshot`. A rel row carrying
+   `snap = 0` is unstamped, and joins to nothing. Distinguishable from a row
+   stamped with repo `""`, which hashes to a nonzero id.
+5. Hash collision: 64-bit content hash over the whole corpus of (repo, rev)
+   pairs. The pair count is bounded by repos times revs scanned, order 10^3.
+   Birthday bound is negligible and this is the SAME exposure `StringId::of`
+   already carries for every interned string in the database, at a far larger
+   cardinality. No new class of risk; a pre-existing one, restated at lower N.
+
+### 3.4 What this does for `read_content`, and for the 0.4 defect
+
+Today `read_content(root, rev, path)` takes a scan root and a bare rev text and
+guesses. With a snapshot in hand the read path stops guessing, because a snapshot
+resolves to `(repo_slug, rev_text)` and `repo_slug` resolves to a repo root via
+`repo_roots()` (`engine/mod.rs:835`), which is the *repo* root, not the scan
+root. The prefix `scan_root.strip_prefix(repo_root)` is then computable, which is
+exactly the missing `bench/flow/` in 0.4.
+
+That fix is NOT free-riding on this arc; it needs `read_content` to take a
+snapshot instead of `(root, rev)`, and every caller updated. It is staged
+explicitly in section 6 rather than folded in silently.
+
