+
+## 4. Where the four layers disagree
+
+The protocol says these layers may disagree. They do, in three places, and the
+disagreements are the actual design decisions. None of them should be resolved
+by an agent.
+
+### 4.1 Signatures say "replace two columns with one". Storage says arity breaks.
+
+`SnapshotId` replacing `(repo, rev)` is arity-preserving for the 12 rels in
+bucket (b) — one `rev` column becomes one `snap` column, and those rels silently
+GAIN their repo half, which is the arc's stated goal, achieved for free.
+
+For the 7 rels in bucket (a) it is an arity REDUCTION: `file(repo, rev, path,
+content)` becomes `file(snap, path, content)`. Every positional `.dl` atom
+against those 7 rels breaks. `file` in particular is the single most-used rel in
+the corpus.
+
+Two resolutions, both defensible, and the choice is the user's:
+
+- **(A) Reduce arity, provide `snapshot(snap, repo, rev)` as a builtin join rel.**
+  Honest 1NF. Breaks every `file(...)` call site in `.dl/`, `examples/`, `book/`,
+  and the test corpus. Large mechanical edit, fully rail-checkable.
+- **(B) Keep `repo`/`rev` as VIEW-projected columns over the stored `snap`.**
+  `rel_file` stores `snap`; `rel_file_txt` (the existing `_txt` view layer)
+  joins `_snapshot` and projects `repo`, `rev` back out. Zero surface breakage,
+  zero `.dl` edits. The cost is that the view layer, not the table, is what the
+  DSL sees, and a positional atom's column list is then computed rather than
+  declared.
+
+(B) preserves the surface and still gets the storage win and the scoped delete,
+because deletes hit the table. (A) is the cleaner model. I do not think an agent
+should pick; the surface-compatibility cost of (A) is a product decision.
+
+### 4.2 Storage says "one column". The DSL surface has no literal for it.
+
+A `Type::Rev` literal works because `"WORK"` and a sha are text the user can
+write, and `lower.rs:38` compiles a text literal in an interned position to
+`StringId::of(s).sqlite()` at compile time, no lookup. A snapshot has no natural
+text spelling. The only spellings are:
+
+- `"repo@rev"` — a `format!`-composite string, which is precisely what the arc's
+  own law bans. Rejected, even as surface-only, because it would immediately be
+  parsed back apart somewhere.
+- A `snap(Repo, Rev)` term function that lowers to the same domain-separated
+  hash at compile time. This is the coherent answer: the surface keeps two
+  terms, storage keeps one column, and the mapping is a compile-time function
+  with no round trip. It is also more machinery than the rest of the arc.
+
+Layer-1 signatures assume a snapshot is minted in Rust and never written by a
+user. That is true for every engine-emitted row and false for a hand-written
+`.dl` fact. Section 6 stages the term function separately.
+
+### 4.3 Lifetimes say "cache across ticks". Correctness says "not across a commit".
+
+`SnapshotId` is a pure function of its inputs, so it is safely cacheable
+forever. `RevId` is not: `WorktreeRev` is deliberately cleared at each tick top
+and on a git event (`revid.rs:157-166`), because a tick spanning `git commit`
+must use ONE oid throughout. The snapshot inherits that constraint from its rev
+half, not from itself. Any snapshot cache must therefore key on the RESOLVED rev
+and be seeded downstream of `WorktreeRev`, never memoize `(repo_slug, "WORK")`.
+Since `SnapshotId::of` takes a `&RevId` and `RevId::parse` refuses the alias
+(`revid.rs:96-110`), the type system already blocks the wrong version. Worth
+stating because a naive `HashMap<(String,String), SnapshotId>` keyed on the
+pre-resolution text would reintroduce the bug a0a0ff25 fixed.
+
